---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import { u } from "../lib/url.js";
---
<Base title="Argentina"
      eyebrow="Rosario, Santa Fe — and the records that do not answer"
      dek="Rosario is the place the family names. Nothing is yet proved there — but Rosario is a good place to be sent, because its records survive and are largely reachable.">
<section class="blk">

<div class="panel" style="margin:1.4rem 0">
    <p style="margin-top:0"><span class="chip doc">Confirmed, 8 September 2026</span></p>
    <p style="margin-bottom:0">
      <strong>Pablo Armando Lerena was born at Rosario de Santa Fe on 22 February 1882</strong>, and was
      Argentine by birth, not naturalised — from the passport he carried, signed and thumb-printed.
      The page below was written before that document surfaced. The tradition it describes was correct.
      <a href={u("/passport")}>The passport &rarr;</a>
    </p>
  </div>
</section>
<section class="blk">
<h2>What was claimed</h2>
  <p>
    <span class="chip lore">Family testimony</span> David Defranceski's great-grandfather — the grandfather
    of <strong>Cheryl Anne Lerena</strong> — came from Argentina, and <strong>Rosario</strong> is the city
    associated with him. He is said to have gone to South Africa as <strong>Consul General</strong>.
  </p>
  <p>
    No name, no dates and no document are attached to this yet. Establishing the name is the first task of
    the archive, and everything else waits on it.
  </p>
</section>
<section class="blk">
<h2>Why Rosario is plausible</h2>
  <p>
    <span class="chip doc">Documented</span> The name's distribution supports the story independently of
    the story. <strong>Argentina holds 293 Lerenas and Uruguay 431</strong> — and Uruguay's density,
    1 in 7,962, is the highest in the world. The <strong>River Plate is the modern heartland of the
    surname</strong>. A family tradition pointing to Argentina is pointing exactly where the name actually
    concentrates, which is a modest but real corroboration. <a href={u("/name")}>The name &rarr;</a>
  </p>
  <p>
    Rosario itself fits the period. Argentina's second city and the great grain port of the Paraná, it grew
    through the late nineteenth and early twentieth centuries on European immigration and on the pampas
    trade — cattle, grain, and <strong>horses</strong>. A family with standing enough to produce a consul,
    and interests in bloodstock, is a recognisable Rosario type of that era.
  </p>
</section>
<section class="blk">
<h2>Uruguay is not a detour</h2>
  <p>
    The River Plate is one social world with a border through it, and families crossed it constantly.
    Given that <strong>Uruguay has the densest Lerena population on earth</strong>, and that the Argentine
    Stud Book's co-founder carried the Uruguayan double surname <strong>Lerena Lenguas</strong>, the
    archive treats <strong>Montevideo as a live possibility from the outset</strong>, not as a fallback
    after Argentina fails.
  </p>
  <p class="cap">
    Stated plainly so it is not forgotten later: if the Argentine search stalls, the correct next move is
    Uruguay, not a harder Argentine search.
  </p>
</section>
<section class="blk">
<h2>Where the records are</h2>
  <p>These are the seams to work, in the order they are worth working.</p>

  <div class="grid g2">
    <div class="panel">
      <h3 style="margin-top:0">Civil registration, Santa Fe province</h3>
      <p style="margin-bottom:0">
        Argentina's civil registration begins in the 1880s. Rosario sits in <strong>Santa Fe</strong>
        province. Births, marriages and deaths from that point should name parents outright — the single
        most efficient way up a generation.
      </p>
    </div>
    <div class="panel">
      <h3 style="margin-top:0">Catholic parish registers</h3>
      <p style="margin-bottom:0">
        Before civil registration, and alongside it, the parishes. Rosario's older parishes and the
        Archdiocese of Rosario hold baptisms, marriages and burials; many are filmed by FamilySearch.
      </p>
    </div>
    <div class="panel">
      <h3 style="margin-top:0">Immigration and passenger records</h3>
      <p style="margin-bottom:0">
        The <strong>CEMLA</strong> immigrant database and the Argentine <em>listas de pasajeros</em> record
        arrivals into Buenos Aires with age, origin, occupation and often the town in Spain. If the family
        arrived from Spain in the emigration century, this is where the Iberian origin is named.
      </p>
    </div>
    <div class="panel">
      <h3 style="margin-top:0">Outbound to South Africa</h3>
      <p style="margin-bottom:0">
        The departure matters as much as the arrival. A consular posting leaves a paper trail at both ends —
        see <a href={u("/south-africa")}>South Africa</a>.
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>
<section class="blk">
<h2>The consul: how it gets proved</h2>
  <p>
    A Consul General is a public officer, and public officers are published. This claim should be among the
    <em>easiest</em> in the archive to settle, once a name exists:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>The Argentine <strong>Ministerio de Relaciones Exteriores</strong> published annual
      <em>Memorias</em> and diplomatic lists naming every consular officer and post.</li>
    <li>The <strong>Boletín Oficial</strong> of the Argentine Republic records appointments.</li>
    <li>On the receiving side, South African and British colonial <strong>government gazettes</strong>
      published the grant of an <em>exequatur</em> — the host state's formal recognition of a foreign
      consul. This is the crispest single document available, and it is dated.</li>
    <li>Contemporary newspapers on both sides reported consular arrivals and social life. Argentine and
      South African digitised press are both searchable.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    Note one caution to carry from the start: <strong>consuls were often honorary</strong>. A merchant
    resident in the host country could hold the post. If the great-grandfather was an honorary consul
    rather than a career diplomat, he may appear in the gazettes but not in the Argentine foreign-service
    lists — and that would also explain a man who bred horses and farmed ostriches while holding a
    diplomatic title. <span class="chip inf">A hypothesis, recorded early so it can be tested</span>
  </p>
</section>
<section class="blk">
<h2>What this page needs next</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>His parents.</strong> The name, the city and the day are now known — Pablo Armando Lerena,
    Rosario, 22 February 1882. What is not known is who his father and mother were. His
    <strong>baptism at Rosario</strong> in the spring of 1882 would name them, and it is now an exact
    target rather than a region.
    <a href={u("/open-questions")}>Open questions &rarr;</a>
  </p>
</section>
</Base>
